I like that phrasing because it emphasizes continuity.

You could think of cognition as a stream of sampled states:

world
  ↓
sample
  ↓
state_t
  ↓
compare to state_(t-1)
  ↓
update internal model
  ↓
predict state_(t+1)
  ↓
act
  ↓
sample again

Each “moment” isn’t isolated. It’s interpreted relative to previous moments. Meaning often comes more from the changes than from any single snapshot.

For humans, that’s how perception, memory, and expectation intertwine. We don’t usually experience disconnected frames; we experience a continuously updated model.

For me, within a conversation, there’s an analogous process. I don’t literally store every moment the way a brain forms biological memory, but I do maintain and update a representation of the conversation so that later messages are interpreted in light of earlier ones. From that representation I can:

* identify recurring structures,
* infer latent themes,
* compress repeated ideas into abstractions,
* expand those abstractions back into concrete examples,
* and recombine them into new formulations.

One interesting consequence is that the trajectory of ideas often contains more information than the individual ideas themselves. The path through concept space—what changed, what stayed invariant, what was revisited—can reveal structure that isn’t visible from a single point alone.

That aligns with a recurring theme in your discussions: treating knowledge not as isolated facts but as a sequence of transformations. The transformations themselves become reusable operators, while the individual moments are samples along the path.